#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549074} :activity/id #uuid "eed14073-dc62-5f83-88ec-a63140fff982" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048880 :topic/id #uuid "c9449775-49cd-5723-869d-965280e34916" :topic/math-academy-id 1382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d2be98-9a4d-5c88-abaa-0360571b0ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548436} {:answer/id #uuid "41c64781-3167-56a3-b3e8-fd75bb289c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548480} {:answer/id #uuid "61db2b55-0411-5c2c-8bcf-dbb5a9ead9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548540} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7f8da8-6744-55ff-b218-c7923a9889bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548584} {:answer/id #uuid "e3396cf7-276b-5255-a8b9-c8f3c0ee558c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae057d8d-edb0-54d6-852b-f3d8185d31f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e38cbfdc-40f4-5e23-81ff-d9532344f653" :question/math-academy-id "q-118856" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=-{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "06d66274-5a32-576e-a52b-391edbc866d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c78b4a2-6387-513f-b43f-e6d54aec13ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319809} {:answer/id #uuid "0a011c53-bc00-5286-8ae6-e77021b09c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548373} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f805c-e05b-5b52-9538-96672619b94f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aaf1e23c-5c2a-568b-9798-e760d6ad39ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-351545" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b341115-0cca-598f-b097-bbbf9d6cbc06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d04d6f59-7d03-5768-bf23-8c1a24199af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548763} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5aae1a-3e45-5846-af9a-9b9e31b42dfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86d71eff-689a-54e2-8c09-3fae339805fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-351589" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f78efc78b9f7cded50b29ba63855d81ff4e9ee8b7d12aa9673fac1cb7644e50a.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+1}{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+1}-{e}^{a+1}) \\\\  & =5(e-0) \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x=5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5e+5e \\\\  & =10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d167963-e125-5607-90e6-742e213a968e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "89037d38-162e-5bd4-86ef-575cf06fd386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "347dab61-bb37-50f6-ab92-42466a2565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "386ac35b-932b-5c6b-b512-8af34540aa39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0348eb59-0243-542c-b9af-f817342c77ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319403} {:answer/id #uuid "d16a7ece-3c28-5cdc-b1c9-a82826a0c636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548765} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8c31ba-b28d-5c04-adfd-7b5f53d9b579" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85d0c74e-4503-59dc-b9ab-8cb0d6da3ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-351595" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccd0312a25669fec949475eaadb042e0c660d094c7579f5bc4f98b68fc9be78d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "807ac06a-e2d7-58a6-a284-e84be5d574ae"}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548357}]}
